    "content": "# P2.5 \u2014 Flip the transport clock to sample-accurate\n\nGoal: `Transport` currently advances by wall-clock delta once per render\nframe (`Transport::Tick`, called from the main loop). Make the audio\nengine's sample counter the source of truth for `Beats()`/`Seconds()`\nwhenever it's running, with the existing frame-driven `Tick()` path\nbecoming the fallback for when it isn't (no device, or it failed to open).\nThis is a small, surgical phase \u2014 one file's internals change\n(`src/core/Transport.h`/`.cpp`), plus two call sites in `AudioEngine`. It\nmust land before P3a: note scheduling needs a clock that's safe to read\nfrom the audio thread with sample-accurate position, not a value that only\nupdates once per ~16ms render frame and was never meant to be touched from\nanother thread.\n\n**Clean-room rule, verbatim:** do not open, read, grep, or reference\n`/Users/namansoni/BespokeSynth`.\n\n## Current state, verified\n\n`src/core/Transport.h`/`.cpp` (44/30 lines) is exactly what it looks like:\n`Tick(deltaSeconds)` accumulates `mBeats`/`mSeconds` (plain `double`,\nmain-thread-only), gated on `mPlaying` (plain `bool`) and clamped to avoid\na stalled-frame jump. `SetTempo`/`Tempo` read/write a plain `float mBpm`.\nExternal MIDI-clock sync (`SetExternalSyncEnabled`/`ReportExternalTempo`)\nalready exists and must keep working unchanged \u2014 it only ever touches\n`mBpm` and the freshness-timeout bookkeeping, never `mBeats`/`mSeconds`\ndirectly.\n\nCall sites, all main-thread, all already correct for what they do today \u2014\nyou're changing what they call into, not moving any of them:\n- `main.cpp:8511` \u2014 `Transport::Instance().Tick(ImGui::GetIO().DeltaTime)`,\n  once per render frame, the one real call (every other `.Tick(...)` call\n  in `main.cpp` is a test fixture driving it with a hardcoded delta \u2014\n  leave those alone, they're exercising the same function, not a second\n  code path).\n- `main.cpp:8505-8510` \u2014 feeds `ReportExternalTempo` from\n  `Platform::MidiClockBpm()` once a frame, right before the `Tick()` call.\n  Same pattern this phase should use elsewhere: a background thread\n  (there, Core MIDI; here, the audio render thread) never writes into\n  `Transport` directly \u2014 it publishes into something the consumer polls,\n  or in this case, `Transport` itself exposes an audio-thread-safe entry\n  point (see below), matching the comment at `:8501-8504` about why that\n  pattern exists (\"same pattern as AudioAnalyzeNode/MidiCCNode... rather\n  than the [background thread] callback writing into Transport directly\").\n- `src/main.cpp:7315` \u2014 `AudioEngine::Instance().Start(audioError)`, once\n  at startup, non-fatal on failure (a machine with no audio device still\n  runs). `main.cpp:15611` \u2014 `AudioEngine::Instance().Stop()`, once at\n  shutdown. These are the two points where `Transport` needs to be told\n  the audio clock became available or went away.\n\n## Design \u2014 resolved, not left open\n\n**One signal decides which clock source `Beats()`/`Seconds()` trust:\nwhether the audio sample rate is known.** No separate \"audio driven\" bool\nto keep in sync with it \u2014 `mAudioSampleRate > 0.0` *is* \"the engine is\nopen and its sample counter is live.\" This collapses what would otherwise\nbe two flags that could disagree into one.\n\n`Transport.h` additions:\n```cpp\n// Called once, main thread, immediately after AudioEngine::Instance().Start()\n// succeeds. Captures the current frame-driven position as the audio clock's\n// starting offset, so the switch is seamless - no jump at the exact moment\n// this phase's build lands and the engine starts producing real callbacks.\nvoid NotifyAudioEngineStarted(double sampleRate);\n\n// Called once, main thread, immediately after AudioEngine::Instance().Stop().\n// Freezes mBeats/mSeconds at the audio clock's last live value so Tick()'s\n// fallback path continues from exactly where the audio clock left off.\nvoid NotifyAudioEngineStopped();\n\n// Called from the real-time audio thread, once per AudioEngine::Process\n// callback, before any node in that block reads Beats()/Seconds(). Adds\n// numFrames to the sample counter only while playing - this is what makes\n// pause freeze the audio-driven clock the same way it already freezes the\n// frame-driven one.\nvoid AdvanceAudioClock(int numFrames);\n```\n\nEvery field `AdvanceAudioClock`/`Beats`/`Seconds` touch becomes atomic,\nsince those three can now be called from the audio thread while `Tick`/\n`SetTempo`/etc. are called from the main thread, concurrently:\n```cpp\nstd::atomic<bool> mPlaying { true };            // was plain bool\nstd::atomic<float> mBpm { 120.0f };              // was plain float\nstd::atomic<uint64_t> mAudioSampleCounter { 0 };\nstd::atomic<double> mAudioSampleRate { 0.0 };    // 0 = fallback mode\nstd::atomic<double> mAudioBeatsOffset { 0.0 };\nstd::atomic<double> mAudioSecondsOffset { 0.0 };\n```\n`mBeats`/`mSeconds` themselves **stay plain `double`, not atomic** \u2014 they\nare only ever touched by `Tick()`, which only runs on the main thread and\nonly writes them while `mAudioSampleRate == 0` (fallback mode); the audio\nthread never reads or writes them, so they don't need to be. Getting this\nsplit right (which fields cross threads vs. which stay main-thread-only)\nis the actual point of this phase \u2014 don't reach for `std::mutex` here, the\nwhole design is atomics-only per the audio-thread rules P1 already\nestablished.\n\n`Seconds()`/`Beats()` (`memory_order_relaxed` throughout \u2014 this is a\npolling clock read, not a handoff that needs acquire/release ordering\nanywhere):\n```cpp\ndouble Transport::Seconds() const\n{\n   const double sr = mAudioSampleRate.load(std::memory_order_relaxed);\n   if (sr > 0.0)\n      return mAudioSecondsOffset.load(std::memory_order_relaxed) +\n             (double)mAudioSampleCounter.load(std::memory_order_relaxed) / sr;\n   return mSeconds;\n}\n\ndouble Transport::Beats() const\n{\n   const double sr = mAudioSampleRate.load(std::memory_order_relaxed);\n   if (sr <= 0.0)\n      return mBeats;\n   const double secOffset = mAudioSecondsOffset.load(std::memory_order_relaxed);\n   return mAudioBeatsOffset.load(std::memory_order_relaxed) +\n          (Seconds() - secOffset) * (mBpm.load(std::memory_order_relaxed) / 60.0);\n}\n```\nBoth are then safe to call from **either** thread at **any** time \u2014 no\ndependency on `Tick()` having run this frame, which matters because the\naudio thread's block rate (~93 callbacks/sec at the P0-measured ~470-frame\nblocks) has nothing to do with the render frame rate `Tick()` is driven\nby, and P3a's note scheduling will call `Beats()` from inside\n`ProcessBlock`.\n\n`Tick()` becomes exactly what the plan calls it \u2014 the engine-stopped\nfallback:\n```cpp\nvoid Transport::Tick(float deltaSeconds)\n{\n   if (deltaSeconds > 0.25f)\n      deltaSeconds = 0.25f;\n\n   // unchanged: external-clock-freshness aging runs regardless of mode\n   if (mExternalSync && mExternalClockFresh) { /* ...as today... */ }\n\n   if (mAudioSampleRate.load(std::memory_order_relaxed) > 0.0)\n      return; // audio-driven: Beats()/Seconds() compute live, nothing to accumulate here\n\n   if (!mPlaying.load(std::memory_order_relaxed))\n      return;\n   mSeconds += deltaSeconds;\n   mBeats += deltaSeconds * (mBpm.load(std::memory_order_relaxed) / 60.0);\n}\n```\n\n`NotifyAudioEngineStarted`/`NotifyAudioEngineStopped` implementation \u2014\norder matters in both, publish `mAudioSampleRate` **last** in Started (so\nno reader can observe a live sample rate before the offsets it depends on\nare set) and **first** in Stopped's inverse (capture the live value before\nswitching back to fallback, not after):\n```cpp\nvoid Transport::NotifyAudioEngineStarted(double sampleRate)\n{\n   mAudioSecondsOffset.store(mSeconds, std::memory_order_relaxed);\n   mAudioBeatsOffset.store(mBeats, std::memory_order_relaxed);\n   mAudioSampleCounter.store(0, std::memory_order_relaxed);\n   mAudioSampleRate.store(sampleRate, std::memory_order_relaxed); // publish last\n}\n\nvoid Transport::NotifyAudioEngineStopped()\n{\n   mSeconds = Seconds(); // read the still-live audio-driven value...\n   mBeats = Beats();\n   mAudioSampleRate.store(0.0, std::memory_order_relaxed); // ...then switch back to fallback\n}\n```\n`AdvanceAudioClock`:\n```cpp\nvoid Transport::AdvanceAudioClock(int numFrames)\n{\n   if (mPlaying.load(std::memory_order_relaxed))\n      mAudioSampleCounter.fetch_add((uint64_t)numFrames, std::memory_order_relaxed);\n}\n```\n\n`Rewind()` needs one addition: it currently zeroes `mBeats`/`mSeconds`\nonly, which does nothing visible while audio-driven (those fields aren't\nbeing read in that mode). Also reset `mAudioSampleCounter` and both\noffsets to `0`:\n```cpp\nvoid Rewind()\n{\n   mBeats = 0.0;\n   mSeconds = 0.0;\n   mAudioSampleCounter.store(0, std::memory_order_relaxed);\n   mAudioBeatsOffset.store(0.0, std::memory_order_relaxed);\n   mAudioSecondsOffset.store(0.0, std::memory_order_relaxed);\n}\n```\nThis can race a concurrent in-flight `AdvanceAudioClock` fetch_add from\nthe audio thread (rewind called from the main thread at the same instant a\ncallback is running) \u2014 worst case is one block's worth (~10ms) of position\nerror on the sample a rewind lands on, which is inaudible and not worth\na lock for. Say so in a comment next to `Rewind()` rather than leaving it\nunexplained if someone goes looking for why this isn't perfectly atomic\nacross the three stores.\n\n## Wiring into `AudioEngine`\n\n- `src/audio/AudioEngine.cpp`, `Start()` (currently `:34-41`): on success,\n  after `mSampleRate.store(sampleRate, ...)`, call\n  `Transport::Instance().NotifyAudioEngineStarted(sampleRate)`. Needs\n  `#include \"core/Transport.h\"` \u2014 check the actual relative include path\n  other `src/audio/` files use for `src/core` headers (this codebase's\n  `target_include_directories` already lists `src/core` as a bare include\n  root per `CMakeLists.txt:107-112`, so `#include \"Transport.h\"` without a\n  `core/` prefix is likely correct \u2014 confirm against how an existing\n  `src/audio/*.cpp` already includes something from `src/platform`\n  (`AudioEngine.cpp` already does `#include \"platform/Platform.h\"` at\n  `:5` \u2014 note that one *does* use a directory prefix, so check whether\n  `Transport.h` needs the same or not before assuming; the include layout\n  isn't perfectly uniform across this codebase, verify rather than guess).\n- `Stop()` (`:43-46`): call `Transport::Instance().NotifyAudioEngineStopped()`\n  before or after `Platform::AudioDeviceClose()` \u2014 after is slightly safer\n  (no more callbacks can fire once the device is closed, so nothing can\n  race the notify), prefer that order.\n- `Process()` (`:90-129`): call `Transport::Instance().AdvanceAudioClock(numFrames)`\n  once per callback, after the FTZ/DAZ guard and before the node-processing\n  loop \u2014 so any node whose `ProcessBlock` this callback runs (once P3a adds\n  note-scheduling nodes) sees the position already advanced for *this*\n  block, not last block's.\n\n## What this phase does not need to do\n\n- No new `INode`/node-editor work \u2014 this doesn't touch `main.cpp`'s\n  wiring/cable code from P2 at all, only the two `AudioEngine::Instance()`\n  call sites and `Transport` itself.\n- No UI for showing \"audio-driven vs. fallback\" mode \u2014 nothing user-facing\n  changes; this is purely an internal precision/thread-safety upgrade. If\n  you want a cheap sanity signal for testing (see below), a `printf` in a\n  test fixture is enough, don't add persistent UI for it.\n- Sample-accurate note scheduling itself (reading `Beats()` inside a note\n  node's `ProcessBlock` to decide exactly which sample within the block a\n  note lands on) is P3a's job \u2014 this phase only makes `Beats()` safe and\n  correct to call that way, it doesn't add any caller that does yet.\n\n## Build and verify\n\n```bash\ncmake --build build -j\"$(sysctl -n hw.ncpu)\"\n```\n\nThen two checks:\n\n1. **`INFINITE_DSPTEST`** \u2014 should be unaffected (it calls\n   `AudioEngine::ProcessOffline` directly, never `Start()`/`Stop()`, so\n   `Transport` never leaves fallback mode during that test):\n   ```bash\n   INFINITE_DSPTEST=1 build/Infinite.app/Contents/MacOS/Infinite\n   ```\n   All checks from P1b must still read `OK`.\n\n2. **A new manual/fixture check this phase should add**, since nothing in\n   the existing suite exercises `Transport` switching modes: spawn the app\n   normally (audio device opens via `main.cpp:7315`), and add a small\n   `INFINITE_TRANSPORTCLOCKTEST`-style fixture (same `getenv(\"INFINITE_...\")`\n   convention as every other check) that: reads `Transport::Instance().Seconds()`\n   a few frames after startup and asserts it's advancing smoothly (not\n   frozen, not jumping) once the audio engine is confirmed running; if\n   feasible within a headless-ish test, also verify pausing\n   (`Transport::Instance().SetPlaying(false)`) actually freezes it. If a\n   fully headless version of this check isn't practical given the audio\n   engine needs a real device, say so plainly and describe what you\n   verified manually instead (e.g. watching `Seconds()` printed once a\n   frame for a few seconds via a debug `printf` gated behind the same env\n   var) rather than skipping verification of this phase's actual point.\n\nRun the full hygiene suite too, since this phase touches `main.cpp` (the\ntwo `AudioEngine::Instance()` call sites) even though it's a small touch:\n```bash\n.claude/skills/run-infinite-hygiene/driver.sh\n```\nExpect the same result P2 left this suite in: 39/40 passing, with\n`PHASEATEST` (`\"Smooth\"` node-name collision) as the one known, unrelated,\nalready-flagged failure \u2014 not something to fix in this session, and not\nsomething this phase's changes could plausibly affect (it's a `main.cpp`\nnode-registration check with zero relationship to `Transport`). If\nanything **else** newly fails, that's a real regression from this phase's\nchanges and must be fixed before considering it done.\n\n## Out of scope (explicitly deferred)\n\n- Making tempo-synced audio nodes (a Delay with a \"1/4 note\" mode, P3c) \u2014\n  this phase only makes the clock they'll read correct and thread-safe, it\n  doesn't add any node that reads it yet.\n- MIDI Clock *output* (Infinite driving an external device's tempo) \u2014 only\n  inbound sync exists today (`ReportExternalTempo`), unchanged by this\n  phase.\n- Any change to how `mBpm`'s *external sync* path works\n  (`SetExternalSyncEnabled`/`ReportExternalTempo`/the freshness timeout) \u2014\n  only its storage type changes (`float` \u2192 `std::atomic<float>`), not its\n  logic."
